/-!
# The archimedean local-norm criterion for a quadratic extension

Let `k` be a number field, `L = k(√α)` a quadratic Galois extension of `k` (given abstractly by a
square root `y ∈ L \ k` of `α ∈ kˣ`), `β ∈ kˣ` and `w` an infinite place of `k`.  This file proves
the local-norm criterion at `w`:

  `β` is **not** a local norm from `L` at `w`  ⟺  `α` and `β` are both negative at `w`.

The mathematics is the archimedean splitting of the quadratic extension:

* if `w` is complex then `k_w = ℂ = L_w` and the local norm is the identity, so every element is a
  local norm and the right hand side is false because `IsNegAt` requires `w` real;
* if `w` is real then `k_w = ℝ` and the places `w' | w` of `L` are *all real* when `α > 0` at `w`
  (then `L_w = ℝ × ℝ` and the local norm is onto), and *all complex* when `α < 0` at `w` (then
  `L_w = ℂ` and `N_{ℂ/ℝ} z = z z̄ = |z|²` has image the *positive* reals).

The formal proof isolates the arithmetic of the embeddings: for `w` real and `w' | w`, the complex
number `w'.embedding y` squares to the *real* number `α` evaluated at `w` (`embedding_pow_two_eq_of_isReal`),
and `w'` is real exactly when this square is real (`w'.IsReal ↔ w'.embedding y ∈ ℝ`), which uses
that every element of `L` is `a + b·y` (`aux_exists_eq_add_mul_of_sq_eq`).  Hence
`w'.IsReal ↔ α_w > 0`, and the two possibilities for the local norm (`⊤` or the positive reals)
give the criterion.

The range computations of the two archimedean local norms are
`NK.CFTLib.IdeleHasse.range_localNormInfinite_of_real_complex` and
`NK.NUV2.ArtinBridge.localNormInfinite_range_eq_top_of_real_real`.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false
set_option linter.style.haveILetI false
set_option maxHeartbeats 800000

open NumberField
open scoped NumberField.LiesOver

